No, not necessarily.

Haakons point was more concerning punctuation characters.

Why should a conduct be broken by pricking one or two dots in the dust
( . : ), drawing a simple straight line ( - | / \ _ ' ´ ` ), composite
line figures ( < > [ ] ), curves ( { ( ) } ), a tilted 'x' ( + ), etc.?

One could of course still express semantics with punctuation chars,
like

    . .-.. -... . .-. . - ....

or

    +-- |   +-\ +-- +-\ +-- -+- | |
    |   |   | | |   | | |    |  | |
    +-- |   +-< +-- +-/ +--  |  +-+
    |   |   | | |   |\  |    |  | |
    +-- +-- +-/ +-- + \ +--  |  | |


But since the first would require a technician character class (who
would never go illiterate, would he?), and the second is impossible
to engrave in a single line, and both would not affect any monster
behaviour nor do any magic (just wasting time). [*]

Think of scattering single 'x'es in Bigroom in a way that it forms
the word Elbereth :-)  It would be similar to the ASCII-art example
but would currently *not* break conduct. (And that's ok, IMO.)

I think illiterate conduct would be more appropriate if it considers
*alphanumeric* characters (exceptional case the single 'x') but would
allow engraving punctuation characters to retain the conduct. Since
the command is called "engrave" and not "write" (though the *message*,
when engraving, is about "writing") I think players would wonder less
about the game behaviour, if allowing that.

[*] After all, the illiterate conducts primary purpose is, as far as
I see it, to avoid reading scrolls and books, and prohibit Elbereth.

